          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"sand-la-mare-au-diable-af23d094c2185575\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":2,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.9,\"analysis\":\"Germain directly avows intense love for Marie, so truth-conditional force is dominant. The cascading repetitions of “j’aime,” the wish for several hearts, and the bodily image of happiness overflowing into stomach pain give the speech some independent force as an affective outpouring, not just an emphatic assertion. Its setting immediately before the couple’s marriage creates slight commitment-related resonance, but the words themselves do not clearly undertake or promise anything.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty concerns E: the expansive, apparently overflowing speech supports expressive discharge, although it can also be read as a highly emotional extended avowal. P is only weakly supported by the imminent wedding context.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Germain directly says “je t’aime tant” to his betrothed Marie while they arrive at the town hall for their wedding.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal occurs at a moment of fulfilled domestic and romantic happiness: Germain is riding with Marie and his child in the wedding procession after once believing that Marie would never love him. His love for Marie expands into an exuberant catalogue embracing his children, both families, friends, and seemingly everyone. This framing supports sincerity and emotional overflow. The impending marriage lends the avowal relational significance, but it is not itself a wedding vow or explicit promise.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Germain tells Marie: “Tiens, je t’aime tant.”\",\"supports\":\"A direct, affirmative, present-tense avowal of Germain’s loving state strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He continues through repeated declarations—“j’aime tant ces pauvres petits,” “j’aime tant ta mère et mes amis, et tout le monde aujourd’hui.”\",\"supports\":\"The cascading extension of “j’aime” beyond Marie presents the speech partly as an unfolding affective outpouring, supporting E.\",\"confidence\":0.88},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He says he would need “trois ou quatre cœurs” to contain so much affection and happiness and adds, “J’en ai comme mal à l’estomac.”\",\"supports\":\"The imagery of emotion exceeding the heart’s capacity and becoming bodily supplies positive, though not conclusive, evidence of expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.84},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator identifies Marie as Germain’s “fiancée,” and the exchange takes place “en arrivant à la mairie,” during their wedding procession.\",\"supports\":\"The imminent marriage gives the avowal weak commitment-related resonance but does not make it an explicit undertaking, hence only slight P support.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Germain contrasts the present scene with the earlier time when he believed Marie would never love him and says he is now happy that she loves him and his children.\",\"supports\":\"The contrast frames the declaration as sincere recognition of achieved mutual love and family integration.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the central avowal, while E captures the independently supported quality of emotional overflow and a low P score registers the wedding-related relational resonance. No important feature of the core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
